          "Angular invariance under unit-sphere projection transforms Euclidean geometric ranking into one linear pass across contiguous coordinate pairs. Inside `cosine_similarity(v1, v2)`, dividing `dot` by the product `n1 * n2` ensures uniform magnitude invariance so document length or token count variation cannot bias relevance scores toward repetitive passages stuffed with domain keywords.",
          "Floating-point precision subtle errors creep into vector similarity when ingest workers store embeddings quantized to float16 (`bfloat16` or `float16`) while Python query runners evaluate inner products in IEEE-754 double precision (`float64`). When summing `1,536` small products centered near zero, rounding errors stay tiny individually yet occasionally flip tie orderings unless normalization denominators check non-zero energy strictly.",
          "Cross-version schema drift turns silent coordinate truncation into invisible relevance corruption during zero-downtime model upgrades. If a rolling worker accidentally compares a freshly encoded `256`-dimensional query against an unmigrated `128`-dimensional prefix shard, Python's `zip(v1, v2)` quietly halts halfway through the query slice without throwing an exception unless caller validation asserts matching dimensionality up front.",
          "Margin separation telemetry derived from `cosine_similarity` outputs provides an empirical early-warning signal before shortening prefix dimensions further in production. When the score gap between true positive document pairs (`0.9676`) and hard topically similar negatives (`0.2269`) shrinks abruptly across held-out queries as `prefix_dim` drops below `128`, engineers know immediately where model capacity saturates.",
          "Dot-product calibration against unit norms guarantees query prompt length never distorts cosine ranking inside semantic indexes: multiplying `[0.8, 0.6]` by `[0.9, 0.44]` gives numerator `0.984`, while normalizing denominators (`1.0 * 1.001798`) locks true angular proximity cleanly at `0.9822`, whereas orthogonal candidate `[0.1, 0.99]` computes `0.674` divided near orthogonally down cleanly to `0.2269` vs `[0.8, 0.6]` (`Wait` on `[0.1, 0.99]` vs `[0.8, 0.6]`: `0.08 + 0.594` vs orthogonal `[ -0.6, 0.8 ] = 0.0`—demonstrating exact vector orientation sensitivity)."
