          "Greedy redundancy suppression (`MMR`) resolves top-k overlap directly by picking results iteratively: select the single strongest query match first, then score every remaining candidate using `lambda * query_sim - (1 - lambda) * max_selected_sim`.",
          "Iterative penalty selection in `maximal_marginal_relevance(query, candidates, lambda_param=0.5, top_k=2)` balances novelty against proximity: initialize selected list with top query match (`1`), evaluate remaining items' worst-case similarity penalty against already-selected vectors on step two, select diverse winner (`2` / `3`), and stop at `top_k`.",
