          "In navigational and known-item search scenarios, users seek a single specific document or authoritative factual answer.",
          "Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) evaluates the quality of the first relevant document encountered in the ranked results list.",
          "For a given query, the reciprocal rank is 1 divided by the rank position of the first matching relevant document.",
          "If the relevant document appears at rank 1, reciprocal rank is 1.0; if it appears at rank 2, reciprocal rank is 0.50.",
          "Our compute_mrr function traverses retrieved document identifiers and returns 1.0 / rank for the first matching ground-truth document.",
          "MRR rewards retrieval algorithms that place authoritative answers at the very top of search result rankings.",
          "Recall@K ignores rank position completely within window `K`—treating finding the answer at rank `1` identically to burying it down at rank `10`. Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) zeroes in specifically on how early the very first relevant match surfaces.",
          "Examining `compute_mrr(retrieved_ids, ground_truth_ids)` steps through ranked predictions (`enumerate(..., start=1)`): locate the smallest 1-based rank `idx` whose `doc_id` belongs to `ground_truth_ids`, return `1.0 / idx` (`1 / 2 = 0.5` when doc `102` appears at rank 2), and emit `0.0` if nothing matched.",
          "Where does MRR shine operationally? Navigational searches, question-answering bots, and documentation portals where users click the first valid answer card and ignore positions `3` through `10`.",
          "Averaging reciprocal ranks across `1,000` benchmark queries produces a single sensitive indicator (`0.785 MRR` means answers typically sit right around rank `1` or `2`).",
          "Notice MRR deliberately ignores second or third relevant documents—making it ideal for single-answer lookups while inadequate alone for broad research queries needing multi-document diversity.",
          "Using 1-based ranking (`start=1`) prevents `ZeroDivisionError` at index `0` and ensures rank `1` scores exact maximum `1.0`.",
          "Testing first-position (`1.0`), second-position (`0.5`), and zero-hit (`0.0`) traces validates exact rank scoring.",
          "Binary `Recall@10` scores look identical whether the exact answer sits cleanly at position one or buried invisibly down at slot ten below distracting clutter. Measuring `compute_mrr(retrieved_ids, ground_truth_ids)` scores inverse rank directly (`1.0 / rank`), pinpointing immediately how quickly users spot their first valid match!",
          "Mean Reciprocal Rank guides single-answer search portals—such as navigational shortcuts, support ticket bots, and coding documentation bars—where user click-through concentrates overwhelmingly on slots one and two.",
